AB  - Serverless computing has become an important paradigm for machine learning (ML) platforms as it provides efficient resource management through auto-scaling, cost-efficient, and event-driven execution. However, ML workloads have unique demands in terms of heterogeneous resource requirements, latency sensitivity, and stateful dependencies. Therefore, it provides significant limitations in existing serverless scheduling and resource allocation schemes due to cold-start delays, inefficient scaling, and poor adaptability. To address these challenges, we introduce Rubix, an adaptive and fast-performant scaling scheme for serverless-enabled ML platforms. It leverages workload prediction and profiling to preemptively allocate resources. Then, it employs a heterogeneous resource allocation model to dynamically match compute resources to ML task needs while improving resource utilization and latency. Rubix also incorporates a state-aware scheduling and fast-performant runtime to optimize the ML workflows while accelerating execution through container reuse and framework-specific optimizations. Rubix demonstrates substantial improvements in latency and resource efficiency compared to state-of-the-art serverless platforms.
