AB  - Fog computing can meet low-latency requirements, but its limited capacity makes admission and offloading decisions crucial under variable demand. This paper proposes an auction-based fog admission mechanism for trace-driven fog-cloud offloading. Tasks arrive in discrete windows and are admitted to fog nodes under explicit CPU/memory constraints. Congestion-dependent completion times are estimated using an Erlang-C delay predictor under an M/M/m approximation, while Service Level Agreement (SLA) deadlines are defined in a data-driven way by calibrating slack values from baseline delay distributions. Fog nodes publish ask prices that increase with congestion, and tasks submit bids that combine priority with the expected SLA gain of fog versus cloud; an SLA feasibility gate further protects high-priority tasks. Experiments on five workload segments from production traces show that the proposed mechanism substantially reduces SLA violations compared to a min-delay baseline, uses fog resources more selectively, and yields a meaningful revenue signal reflecting resource scarcity.
