AB  - His vessels are leaky and unsound, and night and day he is compelled to be filling them, and if he pauses for a moment, he is in an agony of pain."Plato, Gorgias, translated by Benjamin Jowett How to read this book Who it is forYou, if you run a database, cache or queue that more than one process writes to at the same time.You have probably seen most of what is in this book happen.What you may not have is all seven properties in your head at once, which is the thing that lets you look at a design on a whiteboard and say "that will lose writes on failover" before anyone has built it.I assume you know big-O, hash tables and balanced trees, processes and threads, TCP at the socket level, and virtual memory at course level.Everything above that line I define the first time I use it.The floor is deliberately low, because the hard part here is not the prerequisites but the fact that these properties are almost never taught together, and knowing four of them separately does not tell you what the fifth will do.If you have never run anything in production, this still works, but you will be taking my word for things you would otherwise recognise.O(N) Big-O notation, describing how a cost grows with the size of its input.Assumed from the background floor and used constantly from Chapter 2 onward.The raised dot in a chapter title, as in "3 • The budget", separates the number from the name and carries no meaning.Diagram conventions are a separate matter and are fixed in Appendix B.
